03:41:16 <elliott> ais523: the ambiguity is easily traceable to one sentence
03:41:24 <elliott> The k "Iterate" instruction pops a value n off the stack. Then it finds the next instruction in Funge-space in the path of the IP (note that this cannot be a marker such as space or ;), treats it as an instruction, executing it n times. This takes only one tick with respect to concurrent operation.
03:41:29 <elliott> ais523: "Then it finds the next instruction in Funge-space in the path of the IP"
03:41:37 <elliott> ais523: is this the next instruction after the k, or the next instruction after the IP?
03:41:41 <elliott> "next" is ambiguous here
03:41:43 <elliott> because of the previous sentence
03:41:57 <ais523> ah, yes
03:42:16 <ais523> wait, isn't the IP always treated as being at the k anyway?
03:42:20 <ais523> so it doesn't make any difference?
03:42:28 <elliott> ais523: no
03:42:30 <elliott> ais523: because the second k
03:42:41 <ais523> oh, ouch
03:42:48 <elliott> does it look at the instruction to the right of the IP (itself, "k") or to the right of the k ("5")?
03:42:58 <ais523> so for kk, the question is which k has its location treated as containing the IP?
03:43:06 <elliott> ais523: apparently, Vorpal special-cases nested "k"s, which suggests to me that his interpretation is probably objectively wrong, so I'm not going to try and find out what it is :P
03:43:10 <elliott> ais523: not really
03:43:13 <elliott> the IP is objectively on the first k
03:43:18 <ais523> indeed
03:43:24 <ais523> and that's true no matter what instruction you execute
03:43:25 <elliott> but the question is whether the sentence tells you to look for the first instruction after the IP
03:43:29 <ais523> e.g. ^ turns upwards from the first k
03:43:29 <elliott> or after the current k
03:43:37 <ais523> so the "current k" is always the first one
03:43:39 <elliott> ais523: oh, and here's some fun for you
03:43:45 <ais523> whether it's executing ^ or k or # or whatever
03:43:47 <elliott> ais523: what happens if you change IP direction half-way through the "k"s?
03:43:52 <ais523> elliott: I'm not sure you /can/
03:44:12 <elliott> ais523: if we go by the next-instruction-after-the-k, then consider 22kk<
03:44:14 <ais523> oh, you mean like 4k] or whatever, nothing to do with nested k?
03:44:28 <elliott> so let's trace this
03:44:31 <elliott> 22kk | stk:
03:44:34 <elliott> erm
03:44:35 <ais523> elliott: "next instruction after the k" is the second k, because "the k" is always the first one
03:44:36 <elliott> i'll put the ip
03:44:40 <elliott> with | before the thing that will be executed next
03:44:45 <elliott> ais523: erm, I mean after the current k
