04:41:21 <Deewiant> y on positive?
04:41:28 <elliott> The y "Get SysInfo" instruction, only available in Funge-98, pops one value off the stack. If the value is zero or negative, y tells you far more than you ever really want to know about the underlying Funge interpreter, operating system, and computer (and is thus usually followed soon after by a n instruction).
04:41:36 <Deewiant> See below.
04:41:48 <elliott> Oh :P
04:41:57 <elliott> That's boering.
04:42:06 <elliott> I wonder what fun fun thing I can make = do.
04:42:17 <elliott> Ooh, maybe interpret it as elisp and start Emacs with it.
04:42:25 <elliott> Or give it to ghci :)
04:43:03 <ais523> y on positive is a pick instruction
04:43:11 <ais523> which can even pick below the sysinfo, if you can somehow work out how big it is
04:43:24 <elliott> wow, that's brilliant
04:43:39 <elliott> most inefficient pick ever?
04:43:49 <Deewiant> Pretty much yes
04:43:52 <elliott> 1 cell containing the number of bytes per cell (global env).
04:43:53 <elliott> aka cell size. Typically 4, could also be 2, 8, really really large, infinity, etc.
04:44:00 <elliott> I like how it states you have to put it in a cell, and then tells you it might be infinity.
