22:59:53 <zzo38> Whose idea was it anyways to make C comments start with /* even though / is a valid binary operator and * is a valid unary prefix operator?
23:00:54 <oerjan> fancy google logo...
23:01:08 <zzo38> At least with C++ comments (also valid in many C compilers) start with // which is an invalid syntax in C, so it is OK that the comment starts with //
23:01:26 <pikhq> zzo38: // comments are C99.
23:01:47 <pikhq> As for why /*? I dunno. The block comment syntax in C is a major misfeature.
23:01:49 <zzo38> pikhq: That is one of the good things they added in C99.
23:01:57 * oerjan vaguely recalls someone saying they removed =- for that reason
23:02:23 <oerjan> and i guess =+ and =* too
23:02:38 <pikhq> oerjan: More like "replaced with something saner".
23:02:49 <oerjan> oh the reverse didn't exist?
23:02:56 <pikhq> Nope.
23:03:28 <oerjan> are there any remaining such ambiguities other that /* ?
23:04:24 <pikhq> Probably, but not many, I'd imagine.
23:04:35 <zzo38> There are really strange things such as (x+++++y) which according to the order of the parser, will always cause it to resolve in an invalid way even though there is a way to split it in which is valid, but that is not quite the same kind of thing.
23:04:47 <pikhq> Unlike C++, where you need to evaluate lambda calculus in order to parse. :P
23:05:10 <oerjan> hm haskell has the same problem with things (although not - which would give a comment) followed by -
23:05:24 <fizzie> There's also plain <, which may be a comparison operator or a start of <<; same goes for - and ->.
23:05:25 <oerjan> which is probably the reason they consider unary - ugly
23:05:43 <pikhq> fizzie: Aaah yes.
23:05:45 <zzo38> fizzie: But in C, < is only a binary operator so < < is not a valid syntax.
23:05:52 <pikhq> zzo38: But << is.
23:06:04 <pikhq> << is also a binary operator. :)
23:06:12 <zzo38> Same with -> because - is not a unary suffix operator so - > is not a valid syntax.
23:06:20 <pikhq> -> is *also* a binary operator.
23:06:24 <zzo38> That is why things like << and -> and // are OK.
23:06:35 <fizzie> It is better in that sense than the /* thing.
23:06:36 <oerjan> zzo38: for +++++ i guess it's better to give an error than to mislead when it's ambiguous
23:06:48 <pikhq> True, it is much much better than /*.
23:06:58 <oerjan> although what about just +++ ?
23:07:00 <pikhq> Especially given the really odd rules for *ending* such a comment block.
23:07:16 <pikhq> oerjan: Ow.
23:07:33 <zzo38> oerjan: I think the specification actually says to treat it as (x ++ ++ + y) but that is wrong because x++ is not lvalue.
23:08:02 <zzo38> Such strange cases are not particularly important, though.
23:08:09 <fizzie> +++ is always ++, +; it's the longest token always.
